<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Almagest</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 150</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Almagest is the astronomical treatise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius-ptolemy">Claudius Ptolemy</a>, written in Greek in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> around 150 CE. It set out the geocentric model of the universe and tables of planetary movements that remained the basis of astronomy until the work of Copernicus. Its Arabic name comes from translations made in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gerard-of-cremona">Gerard of Cremona</a> translated it from Arabic into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> in 1175.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/almagest.jpg" alt="Almagest" width="320" height="456" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ptolemy · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Claudius_Ptolemaeus,_Almagestum,_1515.djvu" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The work was originally called the Mathematical Syntaxis and consists of thirteen books. Book I outlines a cosmology in which the spherical Earth lies motionless at the centre of a spherical heaven, and introduces a table of chords and spherical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trigonometry">trigonometry</a>. Later books treat the length of the year and the motion of the Sun, the Moon and eclipses, the fixed stars, and the five planets that can be seen without a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telescope">telescope</a>, including their stations and retrograde motion. Ptolemy took over Hipparchus' model of the Sun and gave the Moon a further device, then built models for the other planets by adding a third device, the equant, where Hipparchus had failed.</p><p>Books VII and VIII hold a catalogue of 1022 stars in 48 constellations, each given an ecliptic longitude and latitude. It is the oldest star catalogue whose complete tables of coordinates and magnitudes survive. Its longitudes fit the middle of the first century CE better than the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antoninus-pius">Antoninus Pius</a>, which Ptolemy gives as their date. Explanations offered include summation errors from Hipparchus' figure for precession, observations made earlier by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menelaus">Menelaus</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, and a miscalibrated instrument.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The first Arabic translations were made in the 9th century, in two separate efforts, one sponsored by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mamun">al-Ma'mun</a>, who received a copy as a condition of peace with the Byzantine emperor. The Arabic name, al-majisti, comes from the Greek megiste, a superlative of 'great'. No <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> translation existed before the 12th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gerard-of-cremona">Gerard of Cremona</a> finished his from the Arabic in 1175, working at the Toledo School of Translators, and it proved more influential than an earlier version made directly from Greek by Henry Aristippus.</p><p>In the 15th century a Greek text reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a>. Regiomontanus made an abridged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> version at the urging of Cardinal Bessarion, and George of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trebizond</a> made a full translation with a commentary as long as the original, which drew criticism. The geocentric model of the work was accepted for more than 1,200 years, in the Byzantine and Islamic worlds and in Western Europe until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scientific-revolution">Scientific Revolution</a>. Much of what is known of Hipparchus comes from it.</p><p>In 1977 Robert R. Newton argued in The Crime of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius-ptolemy">Claudius Ptolemy</a> that Ptolemy had invented or altered his observations. Bernard R. Goldstein and others rejected the charge of fraud, and Owen Gingerich, while noting some fishy numbers, did the same.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Almagest” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…al-Andalus to fall, and in Toledo, where Archbishop Raymond (1125–1152) encouraged the work, scholars such as Gerard of Cremona and Dominicus Gundissalinus translated Arabic works of philosophy, astronomy and medicine into Latin, including Ptolemy's <b>Almagest</b> and works of Avicenna and al-Farabi, transmitting them to the schools of Europe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-castile">Kingdom of Castile · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Claudius Ptolemy wrote the <b>Almagest</b> and the Geography there in the second century, Plotinus studied under the philosopher Ammonius Saccas before founding Neoplatonism in Rome, and Hero of Alexandria described machines and devices.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-egypt">Roman Egypt · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Almagest</b> of Claudius Ptolemy, written in the 2nd century CE, gave astronomers a geometrical model of the planets, built of circles upon circles, that predicted their positions well, and the writings of Galen governed anatomy and medicine.</blockquote><a href="/movement/scientific-revolution">Scientific Revolution · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>In the twelfth century scholars in Spain and Sicily translated Arabic works into Latin; Gerard of Cremona, working in Toledo, alone rendered dozens of texts, among them Ptolemy's <b>Almagest</b>.</blockquote><a href="/essays/cross-cultural-exchange">Cross-Cultural Exchange</a></li></ul></section>
